item.description:
The latency benchmark stores 32-bit indices relative to one base address:
for (std::size_t i = 0; i &lt; steps; ++i) k = next[k]; On x86-64, a compiler can combine the load with a scaled-index addressing mode. With the base in rdi and the zero-extended index in rax, the essential instruction is:
mov eax, DWORD PTR [rdi + rax*4] The instruction performs one load, but its next address depends on the loaded index and on the fixed base.
